findings.body:
`apply_coupon` previously only read `coupon.amount`; it now unconditionally dereferences `coupon.expires_at` and compares it to an aware datetime. Three reachable inputs break:

- a coupon object without the attribute at all raises `AttributeError` instead of applying;
- a never-expiring coupon with `expires_at = None` raises `TypeError: '<' not supported between 'NoneType' and 'datetime'`;
- a naive `expires_at` (common if it comes straight out of a DB driver without tzinfo) raises `TypeError: can't compare offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`.

The fact that the existing test had to be edited to add `expires_at` is direct evidence that coupon objects were previously constructed without it, so other construction sites are likely in the same position. Guard with `if coupon.expires_at is not None and coupon.expires_at < ...` (or normalise to UTC first), and confirm every producer of `Coupon` now sets the field.
